# BASELINE — migrations that are HISTORY, not instructions.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. A migration is only replayable until a LATER migration changes what it depends
# on. 0017_perf_rpc_consolidation.sql joins on `orgs.personal`; 20260728070000_one_org_per_user.sql
# dropped that column. So 0017 can never run against the current schema — it is not a retryable
# failure, it is a permanent one.
#
# That only matters if a row goes missing from schema_migrations, and one did: before apply+record
# were made atomic (see apply-migrations.sh), an interrupted deploy could leave a migration APPLIED
# BUT UNRECORDED. The atomic fix stops NEW gaps; it cannot repair OLD ones. Staging carried such a
# scar for 0017 and it detonated on 2026-08-01, twice, halting deploys until someone reconciled the
# ledger by hand.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES. Every version listed here is RECORDED rather than RUN — but only when the
# database already has a schema. On a genuinely fresh database they still execute normally, or a new
# environment would come up with a recorded history and no tables at all. That condition is the
# whole safety of this mechanism; see apply-migrations.sh.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. The .sql files stay exactly where they are. They remain the record of how the
# schema got here, and they are still what builds a database from nothing. This only stops them
# being re-executed against a schema that has moved on.
#
# The numbered era is closed — everything since is timestamped — which is what makes this a clean
# line to draw.
